Run processes on a fork-less platform (wasm) via os.posix_spawn().

wasm32-unknown-linux-musl has no fork(): musl omits the symbol because wasm
cannot implement its return-twice contract, so _posixsubprocess (fork_exec) is
not built (py_cv_module__posixsubprocess=n/a). Upstream subprocess falls back to
fork_exec for anything the posix_spawn() fast path does not cover (bare program
names resolved via PATH, close_fds without addclosefrom_np, ...), which cannot
work here. Route every child launch through os.posix_spawn() when fork_exec is
unavailable: tolerate a missing _posixsubprocess at import and force
_USE_POSIX_SPAWN on. Extend os.posix_spawn file actions with musl's addchdir_np
extension, pass setsid/setpgroup attributes through, and emulate closefrom by
enumerating /proc/self/fd in the parent and adding ordered close actions after
the stdio dup actions. Resolve PATH by attempting each candidate with the same
error precedence as os._execvpe(), including bytes executables. Options with no
spawn equivalent (preexec_fn, uid/gid/groups, and umask) raise
NotImplementedError rather than being silently ignored.

The multiprocessing spawn context also calls _posixsubprocess directly to
preserve its control-pipe descriptors. Fall back to os.posix_spawn there too:
dup2(fd, fd) clears FD_CLOEXEC for the descriptors the child needs, and ordered
close actions remove everything else. Advertise only the real spawn context
when os.fork is absent, and let the semaphore resource tracker work without the
mmap-dependent _posixshmem module.

